11068	{"artifactId":"LLD-b7a68147dcf72034-s4","entry":{"id":"review-b35bb96e0cb33dd00396104f679d8c42","actor":{"id":"insrc:review","kind":"system","properties":{}},"timestamp":"2026-10-24T15:07:46.417Z","operation":"review","outcome":{"result":"block","summary":"review: block","data":{"kind":"review","report":{"artifact":"design.story","stage":"design.story","verdict":"block","findings":[{"claimId":"coverage-of-intent.1","ref":"coverage-of-intent","item":"coverage-of-intent","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"The proposed exclusions satisfy upstream s4 ac1.","evidence":"DEF s4 ac1 requires that for 'an operation that git supports', 'the operation is reachable through a named tool for its family'. The LLD excludes terminal, GUI, mail, server and selected plumbing operations. It explicitly describes this as a narrower reading requiring confirmation; the upstream criterion does not contain that exception. [files: .insrc/artifacts/DEF-b7a68147dcf72034.json]","action":"Obtain an explicit upstream scope amendment before treating excluded operations as satisfying ac1.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.1","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"There are 22 existing git registrations, with untyped helper failures and the stated status parser available to extract.","evidence":"registerGitTools registers the 22 named definitions, including gitCherryPickTool. helpers.ts spawnFail and fail return output, format, success and error without failure. status.ts executes '--porcelain=v2', '--branch', '--null' and parses stdout with its local parsePorcelainV2 function. [files: src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/index.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/helpers.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/status.ts]","action":"Preserve these existing registrations and extract the parser as proposed.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"new-versus-reuse.1","ref":"new-versus-reuse","item":"new-versus-reuse","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The existing shared contracts support schema checking, typed calls, per-input approval and fail-safe handling of approval exceptions.","evidence":"call-tool.ts defines TypedTool as Tool with required TSchema inputSchema and outputSchema, and derives input/output types with Static. executor.ts rejects missing or mismatching successful data before hooks. resolveApprovalClass catches approval-function exceptions and returns 'destructive'. shouldAsk always asks for that class. [files: src/daemon/tools/call-tool.ts, src/daemon/tools/executor.ts, src/daemon/tools/approval-policy.ts, src/daemon/tools/types.ts]","action":"Preserve concrete schema types on exported definitions so they satisfy TypedTool; consume the existing executor and approval implementation.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"change-sites.1","ref":"change-sites","item":"change-sites","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The proposed new-tool inventory supplies sufficiently concrete input/output contracts for every covered operation.","evidence":"The LLD delegates the 'exact set' and input shapes to a future coverage.ts and includes unspecified 'worktree-free plumbing'. The current registration module contains only the existing 22 tools; neither it nor the upstream HLD supplies the omitted per-operation schemas, modes or argv contracts. The analyzer also failed during planning with shaper-llm-unavailable, so it supplied no additional inventory. [files: src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/index.ts, .insrc/artifacts/HLD-b7a68147dcf72034.json]","action":"Include the complete coverage inventory and concrete new-tool schemas and operation modes in the design before implementation.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"could-not-verify"},{"claimId":"change-sites.2","ref":"change-sites","item":"change-sites","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"The listed failure change sites are a complete inventory.","evidence":"Beyond the specifically inventoried helpers, branch, status and merge paths, log.ts has inline spawn/nonzero failures, and show.ts has a local spawnFail plus inline nonzero and metadata-parse failures. show.ts also deliberately ignores unsuccessful secondary reads: 'const files = stat.code === 0 ? parseNumstat(stat.stdout) : []', then returns success. A wrapper that only sees success false cannot repair that path. [files: src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/log.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/show.ts]","action":"Inventory secondary reads and all local/inline failures explicitly, including show, log and their parser failures; define propagation of timeout/truncation from secondary commands.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"data-compatibility.1","ref":"data-compatibility","item":"data-compatibility","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"Routing status failures through the shared helpers preserves their existing output text.","evidence":"status.ts currently renders '[git:status] cannot spawn git' and '[git:status] failed:'. helpers.ts interpolates its id as '[${id}]'. Passing the canonical id git_status changes the promised stable prefix; the LLD does not specify retaining the legacy display id. [files: src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/status.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/helpers.ts]","action":"Pass 'git:status' as the display id or separate canonical ids from legacy failure prefixes, and pin both status failure paths in compatibility tests.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"data-compatibility.2","ref":"data-compatibility","item":"data-compatibility","kind":"semantic","severity":"HIGH","premise":"Rejecting every path beginning with a dash preserves existing valid calls.","evidence":"checkout.ts currently constructs ['git', 'restore', '--source', ref, '--worktree', '--', ...paths]. Its unrestricted string-array schema and option terminator allow a literal filename such as '-draft.txt'. The proposed assertSafeArg rejects that valid existing path before spawning, despite the invariant that existing inputs and meanings remain unchanged. [files: src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/checkout.ts]","action":"Validate arguments according to their argv position. Allow dash-prefixed literal paths after '--', or explicitly document and approve the compatibility restriction.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"boundaries.1","ref":"boundaries","item":"boundaries","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The implementation leaves shared pipeline ownership and production caller migration with their assigned stories.","evidence":"HLD storyBoundaries assigns output validation and failure types to s2, approval exchange to s3, git schemas/parsers/destructive choices to s4, and production shell-out migration to s7. The LLD consumes those contracts and its final migration step explicitly leaves production caller moves to s7. [files: .insrc/artifacts/HLD-b7a68147dcf72034.json, src/daemon/tools/types.ts, src/daemon/tools/executor.ts]","action":"Keep the production paths listed under new-tool call sites informational until s7 performs the cutover.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"error-paths.1","ref":"error-paths","item":"error-paths","kind":"semantic","severity":"HIGH","premise":"Classifying git_push as destructive only from force flags catches all destructive calls accepted by the existing input.","evidence":"push.ts accepts an unrestricted ref string and executes 'argv.push(remote, ref)' without restricting refspec syntax. Git's installed manual states that an empty source deletes the destination, with 'git push origin :dev' as its example. Thus ref=':dev' has no force flags and remains common under the proposed classifier, allowing remote branch deletion without mandatory destructive approval. A leading '+' refspec similarly expresses force independently of the boolean flags. [files: src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/push.ts, /usr/share/man/man1/git-push.1.gz]","action":"Classify deletion and force refspecs as destructive, including explicit refspec syntax, and test them through the executor in auto mode.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"error-paths.2","ref":"error-paths","item":"error-paths","kind":"semantic","severity":"HIGH","premise":"Every other new mutation can safely default to common, including git_prune.","evidence":"The LLD explicitly adds git_prune but only names gc prune-now among destructive object-removal operations. The installed git-prune manual says it 'prunes all unpacked objects unreachable ... from the object database'; '--dry-run' is the mode that does not remove anything. This can permanently remove recoverable dangling work, so the default common classification permits work destruction without mandatory approval. [files: /usr/share/man/man1/git-prune.1.gz, src/daemon/tools/approval-policy.ts]","action":"Mark non-dry-run git_prune destructive and audit all new operation modes for irreversible removal rather than relying on a common fallback.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"error-paths.3","ref":"error-paths","item":"error-paths","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The shell result exposes reliable timeout and stdout-truncation signals for the proposed failure handling.","evidence":"ShellResult declares timedOut and stdoutTruncated. runShell sets timedOut before killing the child on timeout and returns stdoutTruncated from 'stdoutBytes > maxBytes' on both spawn-error and close paths. These signals can distinguish the proposed failures without parsing output length. [files: src/daemon/tools/shell-helper.ts]","action":"Check these flags for every primary and secondary command before accepting parsed results.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"tests.1","ref":"tests","item":"tests","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Each upstream s4 acceptance criterion has an identified proving-test category.","evidence":"The actual DEF defines ac1 coverage, ac2 checked results, ac3 destructive classification and ac4 preservation of existing ids/meaning. The LLD maps each to coverage/integration, parser/failure/schema, approval/executor, and snapshot/compatibility tests respectively. The existing approval-class-mapping test already compares every baseline id and serialized input schema, explicitly allowing later additions. [files: .insrc/artifacts/DEF-b7a68147dcf72034.json, src/daemon/tools/__tests__/approval-class-mapping.test.ts, src/daemon/tools/__tests__/fixtures/approval-snapshot.json]","action":"Retain the immutable 228-tool baseline and add separate assertions for new tools. Extend the proposed cases for destructive refspecs, prune and valid dash-prefixed paths identified above; coverage tests must use the approved scope.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"}],"counts":{"high":3,"med":3,"low":6,"unverified":1},"template":"design-spec","reviewedAt":"2026-10-24T15:07:46.417Z","model":"cli-codex:gpt-6.1-sol","reviewedBy":"daemon"},"resolutions":{}}}}}
