396	{"artifactId":"LLD-b7a68147dcf72034-s4","entry":{"id":"update-r5-e7e2f1946f969d96","actor":{"id":"insrc:system","kind":"system","properties":{}},"timestamp":"2026-10-24T15:29:11.848Z","operation":"update","revision":{"number":5,"contentHash":"e7e2f1946f969d960147d84b411c10d4d78f0511171f099300a1d8f51047bbae"},"outcome":{"result":"ok","summary":"revised after daemon review (cycle 2, round 1)"}}}
12557	{"artifactId":"LLD-b7a68147dcf72034-s4","entry":{"id":"review-4ce27380c2f2bc568015a984fa05b685","actor":{"id":"insrc:review","kind":"system","properties":{}},"timestamp":"2026-10-24T15:29:14.675Z","operation":"review","outcome":{"result":"block","summary":"review: block","data":{"kind":"review","report":{"artifact":"design.story","stage":"design.story","verdict":"block","findings":[{"claimId":"coverage-of-intent.1","ref":"coverage-of-intent","item":"coverage-of-intent","kind":"semantic","severity":"HIGH","premise":"One executable case per covered command proves that every supported git operation is reachable, satisfying ac1.","evidence":"DEF §6.4 ac1 requires 'an operation that git supports' to be reachable. The design offers git_symbolic_ref only as {cwd, ref?}->{target}, while the installed Git 2.53.0 command inventory describes symbolic-ref as 'Read, modify and delete symbolic refs'. A read-only case cannot establish coverage of its mutation operations. Similarly, git_reflog explicitly excludes expire and delete without placing them among the pending exclusions. [files: docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E20261023b7a68147/DEF.md, docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E20261023b7a68147/S004/LLD.md]","action":"Inventory supported operations within each command family, provide typed inputs for them, and explicitly seek agreement for omitted operations. Test operation coverage rather than command presence alone.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"coverage-of-intent.2","ref":"coverage-of-intent","item":"coverage-of-intent","kind":"semantic","severity":"HIGH","premise":"Every proposed operation that can overwrite work receives destructive approval.","evidence":"The design classifies git_merge_file as none. The installed 'git merge-file -h' exposes '-p, --[no-]stdout send results to standard output', making stdout an optional mode rather than the default. The design supplies neither inputs nor a requirement to force this mode; ordinary merge-file writes its first file. DEF ac3 requires operations that destroy work to be marked destructive. [files: docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E20261023b7a68147/DEF.md, docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E20261023b7a68147/S004/LLD.md]","action":"Require a non-writing stdout mode for the read tool, or specify the writing mode and classify it destructive. Pin both argv and filesystem effects in tests.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.1","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The registry currently has 22 git tools, and shared git failure helpers omit typed failures.","evidence":"registerGitTools registers the 22 named definitions. spawnFail returns output, format, success:false and error:'git not found'; fail returns the same legacy fields with the selected message. Neither sets failure. [files: src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/index.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/helpers.ts]","action":"Preserve these legacy fields while adding failure metadata.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.2","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"git_show currently ignores the main diff's process failure and falls back to an empty file list when numstat fails.","evidence":"After awaiting diff and stat, execute computes 'const files = stat.code === 0 ? parseNumstat(stat.stdout) : []' and builds a successful result without checking diff.spawnError or diff.code. Header failures are checked separately. [files: src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/show.ts]","action":"Implement the stated main-read failure and auxiliary-read warning distinction.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"new-versus-reuse.1","ref":"new-versus-reuse","item":"new-versus-reuse","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The existing shared contracts support typed calls, input/output validation and fail-safe approval classification as described.","evidence":"callTool accepts a TypedTool with typebox inputSchema and outputSchema and invokes executeTool(tool.id,...). executeTool validates inputs when outputSchema is present and checks successful result.data. resolveApprovalClass catches a throwing function and returns 'destructive'; shouldAsk always returns true for destructive. [files: src/daemon/tools/call-tool.ts, src/daemon/tools/executor.ts, src/daemon/tools/approval-policy.ts, src/daemon/tools/types.ts]","action":"Consume these contracts without duplicating their implementation.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"change-sites.1","ref":"change-sites","item":"change-sites","kind":"semantic","severity":"HIGH","premise":"The new family-tool contracts consistently specify operation selectors sufficient to implement the coverage table.","evidence":"Sections 3.3 and 3.4 disagree: git_stream takes export|import versus fast-export|fast-import; git_maintenance takes run versus maintenance. Section 3.4 adds cat-file|unpack-file, for-each-ref|refs and describe|name-rev selectors absent from their §3.3 input shapes. Existing branch.ts demonstrates that operation selection directly controls the executed command through switch(op). There is no single contract from which to implement these new dispatchers. [files: docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E20261023b7a68147/S004/LLD.md, src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/branch.ts]","action":"Provide one authoritative input/output contract for every family, including every selector and command-specific required argument; derive coverage cases from those contracts.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"change-sites.2","ref":"change-sites","item":"change-sites","kind":"semantic","severity":"HIGH","premise":"The existing shell invocation contract is sufficient for all new tools without an additional input-delivery design.","evidence":"git_apply accepts 'patch (text)', and the new family includes fast-import, mktree and other input-consuming commands. runShell exposes only cwd, env, timeoutMs, maxBytes and signal, and spawns with \"stdio: ['ignore', 'pipe', 'pipe']\". It cannot deliver patch text or a stream to stdin. No temporary-file lifecycle or stdin change is specified. [files: src/daemon/tools/shell-helper.ts, docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E20261023b7a68147/S004/LLD.md]","action":"Specify stdin support and its change sites, or a concrete temporary-file approach with cleanup, encoding and failure handling for each affected tool.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"data-compatibility.1","ref":"data-compatibility","item":"data-compatibility","kind":"semantic","severity":"HIGH","premise":"The legacy-input audit completely identifies newly rejected numeric inputs and correctly states that out-of-range context already fails in Git.","evidence":"git_diff.inputSchema declares context minimum:0 and maximum:50. execute accepts a numeric context and passes it to buildDiffArgv without enforcing that schema; executor currently skips input validation for this tool because it has no outputSchema. Adding outputSchema therefore also rejects context values above 50, beyond the accepted limit/maxBytes changes. The design's assertion that context already fails in Git does not account for this schema-only upper bound. [files: src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/diff.ts, src/daemon/tools/executor.ts, docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E20261023b7a68147/S004/LLD.md]","action":"Audit every existing schema constraint against execution behavior. Explicitly resolve and test the context compatibility change, including context:51, before claiming the accepted changes are exhaustive.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"data-compatibility.2","ref":"data-compatibility","item":"data-compatibility","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The git_log and git_show bound/clamping distinction is accurate.","evidence":"git_log declares limit minimum:1 and maximum:MAX_LIMIT and calls clampLimit. git_show declares maxBytes only as type:number and clamps it with Math.max(...,1024) and Math.min(...,MAX_MAX_BYTES). Executor input validation uses the declared schema, so it will reject bounded log inputs while still allowing show to clamp maxBytes:100. [files: src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/log.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/show.ts, src/daemon/tools/executor.ts]","action":"Retain the proposed rejection and clamping regression cases.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"boundaries.1","ref":"boundaries","item":"boundaries","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The design leaves shared pipeline implementation and production caller cutover with their owning stories.","evidence":"HLD §5 assigns sc2/sc3/sc5 to s2, approval exchange to s3 and direct-call replacement to s7. The existing executor and callTool already implement the shared contracts. S004 migration step 8 explicitly leaves production caller migration to s7, while its family schemas, parsing and destructive classifications match HLD §5.4. [files: docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E20261023b7a68147/HLD.md, docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E20261023b7a68147/S004/LLD.md, src/daemon/tools/executor.ts, src/daemon/tools/call-tool.ts]","action":"Keep caller rewrites and shared pipeline changes outside this implementation, except for an explicitly agreed dependency such as stdin support.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"error-paths.1","ref":"error-paths","item":"error-paths","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"ShellResult provides the signals needed to detect spawn failures, timeouts and truncated stdout.","evidence":"runShell sets spawnError from the child error event, sets timedOut before killing on its timer, and returns stdoutTruncated:stdoutBytes > maxBytes. These are separate from code, which can be null after a signal. [files: src/daemon/tools/shell-helper.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/merge.ts]","action":"Propagate these flags before parsing and before conflict-specific fallback handling.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"error-paths.2","ref":"error-paths","item":"error-paths","kind":"semantic","severity":"HIGH","premise":"Routing all existing non-zero paths through fail(label,stderr,stdout,code,opts) can preserve every legacy failure's output, format and error.","evidence":"git_diff's numstat failure currently returns output '[git:diff] numstat failed: ...' and error 'numstat failed: ...'. Shared fail instead formats '[label] failed: ...' and sets error to the unprefixed process message. git_merge's conflict result is markdown with error 'merge conflict' and structured conflict data. The proposed helper has no rendered-output/error override to preserve these shapes, despite requiring these paths to use it. [files: src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/diff.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/helpers.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/merge.ts, docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E20261023b7a68147/S004/LLD.md]","action":"Separate typed-failure classification from legacy result construction, or extend the helper contract with explicit preservation options. Keep regression fixtures for numstat and conflict paths.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"tests.1","ref":"tests","item":"tests","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The design maps every upstream Story acceptance criterion to a planned test category.","evidence":"DEF §6.4 defines ac1 reachability, ac2 checked results, ac3 destructive marking and ac4 preserved ids/meaning. The LLD acceptance table maps all four to coverage/integration, schema/parser/failure, approval/decline, and snapshot/compatibility tests respectively. The existing approval-class-mapping suite actually reads the 228-tool fixture and deep-compares serialized input schemas, providing the claimed baseline seam. [files: docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E20261023b7a68147/DEF.md, docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E20261023b7a68147/S004/LLD.md, src/daemon/tools/__tests__/approval-class-mapping.test.ts]","action":"Retain the four mappings and extend their cases to address the operation-coverage, destructive-mode and compatibility gaps above.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"}],"counts":{"high":6,"med":0,"low":7,"unverified":0},"template":"design-spec","reviewedAt":"2026-10-24T15:29:14.675Z","model":"cli-codex:gpt-6.1-sol","reviewedBy":"daemon"},"resolutions":{}}}}}
