9700	{"artifactId":"LLD-b7a68147dcf72034-s4","entry":{"id":"review-efd1314166d6db53edc3972663fec79b","actor":{"id":"insrc:review","kind":"system","properties":{}},"timestamp":"2026-10-24T16:12:01.146Z","operation":"review","outcome":{"result":"block","summary":"review: block","data":{"kind":"review","report":{"artifact":"design.story","stage":"design.story","verdict":"block","findings":[{"claimId":"coverage-of-intent.1","ref":"coverage-of-intent","item":"coverage-of-intent","kind":"semantic","severity":"HIGH","premise":"The eleven new read tools provide the capabilities s7 needs to migrate the design corpus reader without changing its behavior.","evidence":"git-reader.ts defines BinaryGitRunner as returning Promise<Uint8Array>, invokes \"cat-file --batch\" with stdin, verifies object framing and recomputes object hashes over the original bytes. Its tests explicitly require preservation of binary bytes and immunity to replacement refs and inherited Git configuration. The proposed git_cat_file returns content:string through the existing text ShellResult and specifies neither binary encoding nor the reader's isolated execution settings. That contract cannot preserve this caller's byte-level behavior. [files: src/design/corpus/sources/git-reader.ts, src/design/corpus/sources/__tests__/git-reader.test.ts, src/daemon/tools/shell-helper.ts]","action":"Design a lossless binary object-read contract, including execution isolation, limits and cancellation, and include the existing corpus-reader regression tests in the s7 handoff.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.1","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The registry currently registers 22 git tools, and shared spawnFail/fail helpers return failures without ToolResult.failure.","evidence":"registerGitTools registers 22 definitions, including gitAmendTool and gitCherryPickTool. spawnFail returns output, format, success:false and error:'git not found'; fail returns the same fields with the command message. Neither sets failure. [files: src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/index.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/helpers.ts]","action":"Preserve the existing ids and legacy helper text when attaching typed failures.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.2","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"git_show currently ignores failure of its main diff read and treats a nonzero numstat read as an empty file list.","evidence":"After awaiting diff and stat, execute uses \"const files = stat.code === 0 ? parseNumstat(stat.stdout) : []\" and returns success:true without checking diff.code, diff.spawnError or diff.timedOut. [files: src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/show.ts]","action":"Implement the documented main-read failure and secondary-read warning distinction.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"new-versus-reuse.1","ref":"new-versus-reuse","item":"new-versus-reuse","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"git_remote list already exposes fetchUrl and pushUrl for reuse.","evidence":"The list operation runs \"git remote -v\", calls parseRemotes and returns data:{op,remotes}. GitRemoteEntry contains name, fetchUrl and pushUrl; parseRemotes populates both URL fields. [files: src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/remote.ts]","action":"Reuse this structured result for ordinary remote URL lookups.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"change-sites.1","ref":"change-sites","item":"change-sites","kind":"semantic","severity":"HIGH","premise":"The proposed tool contracts and s7 handoff inventory cover the existing policy-refresh and self-update operations.","evidence":"policy-refresh.ts fetches with '--no-tags', '--no-write-fetch-head', '--no-recurse-submodules', '--no-prune', '--refmap=' and an explicit destination refspec, then writes remote HEAD using symbolic-ref with two arguments. maintenance.ts likewise fetches an explicit branch-to-tracking-ref refspec. Existing git_fetch accepts only remote/all/prune/tags/depth, and this design preserves that input schema. The new git_symbolic_ref is expressly read-only and defers writing to s8. policy-refresh.ts is also absent from the listed handoff sites. [files: src/workflow/team/policy-refresh.ts, src/cli/services/maintenance.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/fetch.ts, docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E20261023b7a68147/HLD.md]","action":"Provide named tools for these precise operations before s7, or explicitly make s7 depend on the necessary s8 capabilities. Preserve the fetch scope and remote-HEAD update behavior.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"data-compatibility.1","ref":"data-compatibility","item":"data-compatibility","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Declaring outputSchema activates the existing executor's input validation, so unchanged schemas will reject the identified out-of-range inputs earlier.","evidence":"executeTool validates inputSchema only when outputSchema is defined and returns a rejected failure before approval or execution on mismatch. git_log declares limit 1..500 but clamps it; git_diff declares context 0..50 and maxBytes 1024..2097152; git_show declares no maxBytes bounds and clamps it; git_fetch declares depth minimum 1 but floors it and adds --depth only when truthy. [files: src/daemon/tools/executor.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/log.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/diff.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/show.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/fetch.ts]","action":"Retain the explicit compatibility tests for these intentional changes.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"data-compatibility.2","ref":"data-compatibility","item":"data-compatibility","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"Every git_diff paths invalid-input check will be intercepted by unchanged inputSchema validation.","evidence":"diff.ts rejects paths when \"path !== undefined\" in addition to checking the array's contents. Its schema independently declares path and paths and contains no mutual-exclusion constraint. A call containing valid path and paths therefore passes the executor's schema validation and still reaches the legacy invalid-input result. [files: src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/diff.ts, src/daemon/tools/executor.ts]","action":"Limit the compatibility claim to schema-forbidden paths values and explicitly preserve the legacy failure for the path-plus-paths combination; add a test for that distinction.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"boundaries.1","ref":"boundaries","item":"boundaries","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Family-specific schemas, parsing and destructive classification consume the existing shared contracts without requiring s4 to redesign their executor stages.","evidence":"HLD sections 5.2–5.4 assign output checking and typed calls to s2, approval infrastructure to s3, and git schemas/parsing/classification to s4. The implemented executor already checks declared schemas and invokes resolveApprovalClass; approval-policy.ts already makes destructive calls always ask and converts a throwing classifier to destructive. [files: docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E20261023b7a68147/HLD.md, src/daemon/tools/executor.ts, src/daemon/tools/approval-policy.ts, src/daemon/tools/call-tool.ts]","action":"Keep changes to classification and git definitions within s4; reuse the existing executor and approval broker.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"error-paths.1","ref":"error-paths","item":"error-paths","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The shell and executor contracts expose the signals needed for the proposed spawn, timeout, truncation and schema-mismatch handling.","evidence":"ShellResult includes spawnError, timedOut, stdoutTruncated, code, stdout and stderr. runShell sets timedOut before killing the process and derives stdoutTruncated from captured byte counts. executeTool rejects successful results with missing or schema-invalid data as schema-mismatch while returning unsuccessful tool results unchanged. [files: src/daemon/tools/shell-helper.ts, src/daemon/tools/executor.ts]","action":"Classify from the original ShellResult before parsing, including conflict paths; attach failures inside git tools because the executor does not validate failed results.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"tests.1","ref":"tests","item":"tests","kind":"semantic","severity":"HIGH","premise":"The proposed coverage table can satisfy its test requiring every registered git tool to have a covered row and runnable case.","evidence":"registerGitTools registers gitAmendTool, whose id is git_amend. The design's complete covered mapping assigns commit to git_commit and contains no git_amend row. Its simultaneous requirements that every registered git tool appear in the table and every recorded command have exactly one owner cannot account for this second tool for the commit command using the stated single-toolId row shape. [files: src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/index.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/amend.ts, docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E20261023b7a68147/S004/LLD.md]","action":"Allow each command row to name multiple tool cases, or otherwise represent operation variants without duplicating command ownership. Add an explicit git_amend executor case.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"}],"counts":{"high":3,"med":1,"low":6,"unverified":0},"template":"design-spec","reviewedAt":"2026-10-24T16:12:01.146Z","model":"cli-codex:gpt-6.1-sol","reviewedBy":"daemon"},"resolutions":{}}}}}
