9115	{"artifactId":"LLD-b7a68147dcf72034-s4","entry":{"id":"review-5131013852cb48962384f3d30523ca5e","actor":{"id":"insrc:review","kind":"system","properties":{}},"timestamp":"2026-10-24T17:34:16.426Z","operation":"review","outcome":{"result":"block","summary":"review: block","data":{"kind":"review","report":{"artifact":"design.story","stage":"design.story","verdict":"block","findings":[{"claimId":"coverage-of-intent.1","ref":"coverage-of-intent","item":"coverage-of-intent","kind":"semantic","severity":"HIGH","premise":"The new git_cat_file isolation preserves the corpus reader's existing isolation guarantees for the s7 handoff.","evidence":"git-reader.ts constructs a fresh environment explicitly excluding inherited GIT_CONFIG_* and alternate object stores, sets GIT_NO_LAZY_FETCH=1 and GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0, and supplies '-c protocol.allow=never'. The proposed isolate contract specifies only replacement-ref suppression, system/global config suppression and LC_ALL. It therefore does not preserve the existing protection against inherited object/config overrides and lazy network acquisition. [files: src/design/corpus/sources/git-reader.ts, src/design/corpus/sources/__tests__/git-reader.test.ts, src/daemon/tools/shell-helper.ts]","action":"Specify the existing sanitized environment and relevant GIT_READ_ARGS protections for isolated cat-file calls, including no lazy fetching; preserve the reader's bounded diagnostics and cancellation guarantees.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.1","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"git_show currently checks numstat status but can return success when its main diff read fails.","evidence":"execute runs diffArgv and statArgv, computes files with 'stat.code === 0 ? parseNumstat(stat.stdout) : []', then returns success: true without checking diff.code, diff.spawnError or diff.timedOut. [files: src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/show.ts]","action":"Implement the designed distinction between a failed main diff and a failed secondary numstat read.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.2","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"git_fetch depth below one can currently run an ordinary fetch, while its input schema declares minimum one.","evidence":"fetch.ts declares depth minimum: 1, computes 'Math.floor(input[\"depth\"])', and adds --depth only under 'if (depth)'. Thus 0 and 0.5 omit --depth. executor.ts checks input schemas only when outputSchema is declared. [files: src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/fetch.ts, src/daemon/tools/executor.ts]","action":"Retain the explicit compatibility-change tests when adding outputSchema.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"new-versus-reuse.1","ref":"new-versus-reuse","item":"new-versus-reuse","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"A new binary subprocess helper is needed to deliver batch stdin and preserve object bytes.","evidence":"runShell returns stdout: string, decodes through onUtf8Data, and spawns with 'stdio: [\"ignore\", \"pipe\", \"pipe\"]'. The corpus reader instead has a BinaryGitRunner accepting Uint8Array input and returning Uint8Array output. [files: src/daemon/tools/shell-helper.ts, src/design/corpus/sources/git-reader.ts]","action":"Add the shared byte helper without changing runShell's existing contract.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"change-sites.1","ref":"change-sites","item":"change-sites","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"The secondary-lookup inventory is complete enough to classify every runShell outcome without changing existing placeholders.","evidence":"helpers.ts also runs symbolic-ref in currentBranch, diff --cached --name-only in stagedCount, and diff --cached --numstat in stagedSummary. Their failures become undefined, 0 and '_(unable to read staged changes)_'. commit.ts consumes currentBranch and stagedCount before mutation; push.ts consumes currentBranch in execution and approval preview. These lookups are absent from the claimed complete inventory, and checking code alone does not classify timeout or truncation. [files: src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/helpers.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/commit.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/push.ts]","action":"Inventory these helper-mediated reads and their callers. Specify failure handling for precondition reads separately from successful-mutation warnings, and make the audit cover helper calls and process flags rather than merely code checks.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"data-compatibility.1","ref":"data-compatibility","item":"data-compatibility","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The existing snapshot can pin legacy tool ids and JSON input schemas while allowing additive tools.","evidence":"approval-class-mapping.test.ts iterates the snapshot and asserts each old id remains registered and JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(live inputSchema)) deep-equals old.inputSchema. Its comment explicitly permits later Stories' tools outside the snapshot. [files: src/daemon/tools/__tests__/approval-class-mapping.test.ts]","action":"Keep the original baseline intact; adapt approval expectations separately and test new ids independently.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"boundaries.1","ref":"boundaries","item":"boundaries","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The design consumes existing schema checking and approval resolution without requiring changes to their shared ownership.","evidence":"executor.ts already validates input when outputSchema exists, validates successful result.data, and reports schema-mismatch. approval-policy.ts catches a throwing approval function and returns 'destructive'; shouldAsk always asks for destructive calls. HLD §§4.2 and 4.4 assign those mechanisms to s2 and s3. [files: src/daemon/tools/executor.ts, src/daemon/tools/approval-policy.ts, docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E20261023b7a68147/HLD.md]","action":"Keep git-specific parsers, classification and schemas within s4.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"boundaries.2","ref":"boundaries","item":"boundaries","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The s8 boundary change has the approved upstream authority claimed by this design.","evidence":"S008/EXT.md supports the proposed split and says s4 keeps existing tools and reads needed by s7, but calls AMD-b7a68147dcf72034-8 'pending approval'. The checked HLD still assigns the full git family to s4 and has no s8 boundary section. These files do not establish approval of the amendment. [files: docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E20261023b7a68147/S008/EXT.md, docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E20261023b7a68147/HLD.md]","action":"Reference the approved amendment and effective upstream artifact or synchronize the checked documentation to that approved state.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"could-not-verify"},{"claimId":"error-paths.1","ref":"error-paths","item":"error-paths","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"The specified fail helper has enough process information to invoke failureKind consistently for every failure.","evidence":"ShellResult includes spawnError, signal, timedOut and stdoutTruncated. failureKind requires a ShellResult and an optional network flag, but fail accepts only stderr, stdout, code and three optional booleans, with no ShellResult or network parameter. This conflicts with the instruction that every runShell call site passes its ShellResult through the shared classification path. [files: src/daemon/tools/shell-helper.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/helpers.ts]","action":"Make fail accept ShellResult and classification context, or explicitly require callers to compute and pass the failure kind. Preserve legacy rendering separately.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"tests.1","ref":"tests","item":"tests","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The design maps all four s4 acceptance criteria to proposed tests.","evidence":"DEF s4 ac1 requires named operation coverage, ac2 checked results, ac3 destructive marking and ac4 existing ids/meaning. The supplied acceptance mapping assigns these respectively to coverage/integration, schemas/parsers/failures, approval tests and snapshot/compatibility tests. The existing approval snapshot test supplies a real baseline for ac4; these are proposed tests, not verified passing implementation. [files: docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E20261023b7a68147/DEF.md, src/daemon/tools/__tests__/approval-class-mapping.test.ts, src/design/corpus/sources/__tests__/git-reader.test.ts]","action":"Implement the mapped tests and add explicit isolation assertions for sanitized inherited configuration and prohibited lazy fetching.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"}],"counts":{"high":1,"med":2,"low":7,"unverified":1},"template":"design-spec","reviewedAt":"2026-10-24T17:34:16.426Z","model":"cli-codex:gpt-6.1-sol","reviewedBy":"daemon"},"resolutions":{}}}}}
