8521	{"artifactId":"LLD-b7a68147dcf72034-s4","entry":{"id":"review-8d284d4df215810353f8576d1aa3e437","actor":{"id":"insrc:review","kind":"system","properties":{}},"timestamp":"2026-10-24T17:36:29.608Z","operation":"review","outcome":{"result":"block","summary":"review: block","data":{"kind":"review","report":{"artifact":"design.story","stage":"design.story","verdict":"block","findings":[{"claimId":"coverage-of-intent.1","ref":"coverage-of-intent","item":"coverage-of-intent","kind":"semantic","severity":"HIGH","premise":"Marking the refs command covered by git_for_each_ref accounts for its supported operations.","evidence":"The installed git is exactly 2.53.0. Its `git refs -h` lists `migrate`, `verify`, `list`, `exists` and `optimize`. The proposed git_for_each_ref shape supplies listing filters only. Marking the whole command covered leaves migration, verification and optimization unreachable, while preventing the assigned-command check from identifying that gap. [files: docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E20261023b7a68147/S004/LLD.md, docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E20261023b7a68147/DEF.md]","action":"Track coverage by operation for multi-operation commands; assign the remaining refs operations explicitly to s8.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.1","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"git_show currently ignores failure of its main diff read and substitutes an empty list when its numstat read fails.","evidence":"Read the complete execute body: after the parallel reads it uses `stat.code === 0 ? parseNumstat(stat.stdout) : []`, never checks the diff process outcome, and returns success true. [files: src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/show.ts]","action":"Implement the stated distinction between main-read failure and secondary-read warning.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.2","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"git_fetch depth 0 or 0.5 currently runs without a depth option despite the schema declaring minimum 1.","evidence":"fetch.ts floors numeric depth and adds the argument only with `if (depth)`. Its input schema declares `minimum: 1`. The executor currently skips input validation for this tool because it has no outputSchema. [files: src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/fetch.ts, src/daemon/tools/executor.ts]","action":"Retain the explicit compatibility tests for the new rejection.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"new-versus-reuse.1","ref":"new-versus-reuse","item":"new-versus-reuse","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The existing shared contracts support checked TypeBox results and definition-based typed calls without redesign.","evidence":"callTool accepts `T extends TypedTool`, derives input and data through Static, and delegates to executeTool. The executor validates input when outputSchema exists and validates successful result.data before running hooks. [files: src/daemon/tools/call-tool.ts, src/daemon/tools/executor.ts, src/daemon/tools/types.ts]","action":"Consume these existing interfaces.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"new-versus-reuse.2","ref":"new-versus-reuse","item":"new-versus-reuse","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"A new byte-preserving shell runner is necessary, while the isolation policy can be reused from the corpus reader.","evidence":"shell-helper.ts exposes a text-only runShell with ignored stdin and UTF-8 decoding. git-reader.ts already implements binary stdin/stdout and defines gitEnvironment and GIT_READ_ARGS, including the exact isolation variables and read arguments listed in the design. [files: src/daemon/tools/shell-helper.ts, src/design/corpus/sources/git-reader.ts]","action":"Extract the existing isolation definitions and preserve the reader's cancellation and bounds behavior during reuse.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"change-sites.1","ref":"change-sites","item":"change-sites","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"The stated complete secondary-lookup inventory and eight warnings-bearing data types cover the existing successful-mutation lookups.","evidence":"stage.ts performs a post-mutation `git diff --cached --name-only`; on failure it assigns indexAfter [] and still returns success. This lookup is absent from the complete inventory, and GitStageData is excluded from the types allowed to gain warnings. revert.ts also calls currentBranch after successful mutation in okResult, while merge.ts does so after a successful abort. [files: src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/stage.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/revert.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/merge.ts, docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E20261023b7a68147/S004/LLD.md]","action":"Inventory these additional lookups and explicitly define warning or documented placeholder handling. Include GitStageData if failed post-state reads must warn.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"data-compatibility.1","ref":"data-compatibility","item":"data-compatibility","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Adding outputSchema activates earlier input rejection even when the serialized input schema stays unchanged.","evidence":"executeTool places validateAgainstSchema(tool.inputSchema, input) inside `if (outputSchema !== undefined)` before approval and execution. Failed validation returns a rejected failure with the documented `input for tool ... does not fit its input schema` prefix. [files: src/daemon/tools/executor.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/diff.ts, src/daemon/tools/builtins/git/stage.ts]","action":"Preserve the documented exceptions and test both executor-mediated and direct execution.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"boundaries.1","ref":"boundaries","item":"boundaries","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The narrowed s4 scope and deferral of the remaining git families to s8 have upstream documentary authority.","evidence":"The tool-registry plan's `s4 split (2026-10-24)` section records approved DEF revision 2 and AMD-8, assigns the long tail to s8, and explicitly retains git_fetch_refs, git_symbolic_ref, byte-safe git_cat_file and runShellBytes in s4. DEF s8 ac5 explicitly combines s4 and s8 coverage. [files: docs/plans/tool-registry.md, docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E20261023b7a68147/DEF.md, docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E20261023b7a68147/S008/EXT.md]","action":"Keep the implementation within that split and reconcile the stale rendered extension text.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"error-paths.1","ref":"error-paths","item":"error-paths","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The shared approval implementation treats a throwing classifier as destructive and denies calls lacking approval.","evidence":"resolveApprovalClass catches classifier exceptions and returns destructive. shouldAsk returns true unconditionally for destructive. executeTool returns denied without invoking execute for denial, mismatched request IDs or a gate exception. [files: src/daemon/tools/approval-policy.ts, src/daemon/tools/executor.ts]","action":"Use this existing failure-safe mechanism and its test seam.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"tests.1","ref":"tests","item":"tests","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The Story's four acceptance criteria have corresponding planned test categories.","evidence":"Compared DEF s4 ac1–ac4 with the LLD mapping: coverage and integration cases address reachability; schema/parser/failure cases address checked results; approval cases address destructive classification; snapshot and legacy-text cases address compatibility. The existing mapping test really compares serialized input schemas and currently asserts the 228-tool baseline. [files: docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E20261023b7a68147/DEF.md, docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E20261023b7a68147/S004/LLD.md, src/daemon/tools/__tests__/approval-class-mapping.test.ts]","action":"Keep the historical baseline immutable or separately preserved when adding new-tool expectations, and strengthen coverage cases for multi-operation commands.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"}],"counts":{"high":1,"med":1,"low":8,"unverified":0},"template":"design-spec","reviewedAt":"2026-10-24T17:36:29.608Z","model":"cli-codex:gpt-6.1-sol","reviewedBy":"daemon"},"resolutions":{}}}}}
